==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|genom||dated}}

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|en|genom}}, from {{der|en|de|Genom}}, {{coin|de|Hans Winkler|nat=German|occ=botanist|in=1920|nocap=1|nocat=1}} as a {{blend|de|Gen|Chromosom||t1=gene|t2=chromosome|nocap=1|nocat=1}}.<ref>{{cite-book|last=Winkler|first=Hans|authorlink=Hans Winkler|title=Verbreitung und Ursache der Parthenogenesis im Pflanzen- und Tierreiche|url=https://archive.org/details/verbreitungundur00wink/page/165/mode/2up?q=Genom|page=165|year=1920|publisher=Verlag Fischer|location=Jena}}</ref><ref name="ODO">{{R:Lexico}}</ref><ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref> {{surf|+suf|en|gene|ome|alt1=gen(e)}}, or a {{blend|en|gene|chromosome|alt1=gen(e)|alt2=(chromos)ome||nocap=1}}. Spelling altered to reflect the surface analysis.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈd͡ʒiː.nəʊm/|a=UK}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-genome.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈd͡ʒiː.noʊm/|/d͡ʒɨˈnoʊm/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Wodencafe-genome.wav|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊm|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{wp}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|genetics}} The complete [[genetic]] information (either [[DNA]] or, in some viruses, [[RNA]]) of an [[organism]]. {{defdate|from 1930}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|year=2012
|month=March-April
|author=[[w:Terry Sejnowski|Terrence J. Sejnowski]]
|title=Well-connected Brains
|volume=100
|issue=2
|page=171
|magazine=w:American Scientist
|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/well-connected-brains
|passage=Creating a complete map of the human connectome would therefore be a monumental milestone but not the end of the journey to understanding how our brains work. The achievement will transform neuroscience and serve as the starting point for asking questions we could not otherwise have answered, just as having the human '''genome''' has made it possible to ask new questions about cellular and molecular systems.
|accessdate=24 May 2013
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170427064626/http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/well-connected-brains
|archivedate=27 April 2017
}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ashley Strickland|title=Mystery condition gives way to bright future for 10-year-old|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2017/12/18/health/avery-reilly-undiagnosed-diseases-network-profile|date=2017-12-18|passage=Because of her significant testing, Avery had had DNA sequencing of her “exome,” the part of her '''genome''' that makes proteins in the body, said Dr. Michael Wangler, a researcher at the Jan and Dan Duncan Neurological Research Institute at Texas Children’s Hospital.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260204043327/https://www.cnn.com/2017/12/18/health/avery-reilly-undiagnosed-diseases-network-profile|archivedate=4 February 2026}}

