==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ǵenh₁-}}
From {{bor|en|de|Gen}}, from {{der|en|grc|γενεά||generation, descent}}, from the {{glossary|aorist}} infinitive of {{m|grc|γίγνομαι||to come into being}}. {{coin|en|Q347272|in=1909}}, in a German-language publication, from the last syllable of {{m|en|pangene}}.<ref>{{cite-book|de|year=1909|author=Wilhelm Ludvig Johannsen|authorlink=Wilhelm Ludvig Johannsen|title=Elemente der exakten Erblichkeitslehre|trans-title=Elements of exact heredity|publisher=Gustav Fischer|location=Jena|url=https://archive.org/details/elementederexakt00joha|page=124|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/elementederexakt00joha/page/124|passage=Darum scheint es am einfachsten, aus Darwin's{{sic}} bekanntem Wort die uns allein interessierende letzte Silbe „Gen“ isoliert zu verwerten, um damit das schlechte, mehrdeutige Wort „Anlage“ zu ersetzen.}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|jēn}}, {{IPA|en|/d͡ʒiːn/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-gene.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːn|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|Gene|Jean}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|theoretical unit of heredity}} {{lb|en|genetics}} A [[theory|theoretical]] unit of [[heredity]] of living [[organism]]s which may take several values and ([[in principle]]) [[predetermine]]s a precise [[trait]] of an organism's form ([[phenotype]]), such as hair color.
#: {{cot|en|phene}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|cistron<qq:often synonymous>}}
#: {{ux|en|'''genes''' that confer resistance to certain infections}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-21|author=[http://guardian.co.uk/profile/karenmcveigh/ Karen McVeigh]
|volume=189|issue=2|page=10|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/law/2013/jun/13/supreme-court-genes-patent-dna US rules human genes can't be patented]
|passage=The US supreme court has ruled unanimously that natural human '''genes''' cannot be patented, a decision that scientists and civil rights campaigners said removed a major barrier to patient care and medical innovation.}}
# {{senseid|en|portion of a DNA or RNA string}} {{lb|en|molecular biology}} A segment of [[DNA]] or [[RNA]] from a [[cell]]'s or an [[organism]]'s [[genome]], that may take several forms and thus [[parameter|parameterizes]] a phenomenon, such as (often) the structure of a [[protein]].
#: {{hol|en|locus<id:portion of a DNA or RNA string>}}
#: {{ux|en|A change in a '''gene''' is reflected in the protein or RNA molecule that it codes for.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=w:Bill Bryson|title=The Body: A Guide for Occupants|publisher=Black Swan (2020)|page=7|passage=A length of DNA is divided into segments called chromosomes and shorter individual units called '''genes'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal |en |year=2025 |accessdate=2025-10-18 |author=''[[w:The Economist|The Economist]]'' staff |title=Primer 1: Nucleic acids and proteins |series=Biology Primers series |journal=w:The Economist |url=https://www.economist.com/interactive/primers/biology/primer-1/ |passage=One way to define a '''gene''' (there are many, and they are not all equivalent) is as the bit of DNA that contains the information needed to make a specific protein or a functional RNA molecule.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251010015518/https://www.economist.com/interactive/primers/biology/primer-1|archivedate=10 October 2025}}

====Usage notes====
In the simplest case and in principle, a gene as a locus (or part of one) is supposed to be the physical reality corresponding to the theoretical gene as a unit of heredity; in practice, things are far more complicated and confused, which is well known and acknowledged. However, these questions are the subject of still very active scientific research, as well as the topic of both scientific and philosophical questions, especially on the real compatibility between both senses of the term.

