|passage=“I’ve missed two lectures already,” remarked Maurice, who was breakfasting in his pyjamas.<br>“Cut them all — he’ll only '''gate''' you.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Thomas J. Schaeper; Kathleen Schaeper|chapter=Yanks and Brits|title=Rhodes Scholars, Oxford, and the Creation of an American Elite|location=New York, NY|publisher=w:Berghahn Books|isbn=9781845457211|page={{gbooks|Y78i5nkBWxgC|52|gated}}|passage=Dons could ring the front bell and be admitted after that hour. But students who returned after midnight or who stayed out all night were fined heavily or “'''gated'''” – that is, forbidden to leave college for several days.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|biochemistry}} To [[open]] (a [[closed]] [[ion channel]]).<ref>Alberts, Bruce; et al. "Figure 11-21: The gating of ion channels." In: ''Molecular Biology of the Cell'', ed. Senior, Sarah Gibbs. New York: Garland Science, 2002 [cited 18 December 2009]. Available from: http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/bookshelf/br.fcgi?book=mboc4&part=A1986&rendertype=figure&id=A2030.</ref>
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[furnish]] with a [[#Noun|gate]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[turn]] (an [[image]] [[intensifier]]) [[on]] and [[off]] [[selectively]], as needed or to [[avoid]] [[damage]] from [[excessive]] [[light]] [[exposure]]. See {{m|en|autogating}}.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[selectively]] [[regulate]] or [[restrict]] ([[access]] to something).
#: {{ux|en|Access to the building is '''gated''' by a security system.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=HarryBlank|work=w:SCP Foundation|title=Not Ready for Prime Time|date=28 September 2024|url=https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/54-not-ready-for-prime-time|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20241002212926/https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/54-not-ready-for-prime-time|archivedate=2 October 2024|accessdate=2 October 2024|text=Lillian walked the halls wearing a shirt plastered with what she assured everyone was a memetic stun agent; it looked just like the kill agent '''gating''' access to the SCP-001 database file, but as she patiently explained to McInnis, in art, context is everything.}}

