===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become gentle.
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:National Geographic|year=1987|month=October|volume=172|number=4|title=North Carolina's Outer Banks|author=Charles E. Cobb, Jr.|page=510
|text=A September morning brought a stiff northeast wind that blew at 30 miles an hour and kept small craft out of the sound. But by afternoon the wind '''gentled''' and the sound smoothed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Kathryn L.M. Reynolds|title=Garland Roses|publisher=Kathryn L.M. Reynolds|isbn=9781301453559|page=226
|passage=“She&#39;s experienced a horrific and nasty scare and is in a state of shock, but otherwise she&#39;s relatively okay.” Conrad replied, his tone at first grim (as he recalled what he&#39;d seen in the family room) and then it '''gentled''' to a more doctorial tone as he directed his next comments to his patient.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[ennoble]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|IV|III|text={{...}} For he to-day that sheds his blood with me / Shall be my brother; be he ne'er so vile, / This day shall '''gentle''' his condition {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|animal husbandry}} To [[break]]; to [[tame]]; to [[domesticate]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Frank Leslie|title=The Killing Breed|publisher=Penguin|isbn=9781440637377
|passage=Yakima could have tried to catch him, '''gentle''' him as Wolf had been '''gentled,''' but having two stallions in his cavvy would lead to a different kind of trouble.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[soothe]]; to [[calm]]; to make gentle.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|author=William C. Loring|title=An American Romantic-realist Abroad: Templeton Strong and His Music|publisher=Scarecrow Press|isbn=9780810827660|page=201
|passage=A hornist, his playing '''gentled''' by perspective, is out of sight within the woods, but his notes are heard through or over the murmuring mix of bird song and breeze in leaves.}}

